  "query_preview": "..\nSaturday: Elevated to critical fire weather conditions are expected \nSaturday afternoon for much of the Inland northwest as an upper \nlevel low slides through southern British Columbia. This will bring \nwidespread breezy west/southwest winds around 10-20 mph with gusts \naround 20-30 mph with relative humidity generally around 12-25%. \nConditions are marginal for a Red Flag warning, but the best overlap \nof conditions will be on the eastern Waterville Plateau and the \nColville Reservation. There will be locally critical conditions for \nthe Columbia Basin and West Plains as well but have opted out of a \nRed Flag for now. A short fused Red Flag warning may be needed if \nconditions exceed expectations. \n\nSunday through next Saturday: Confidence is high for a stretch of \nabove normal temperatures in the 90s and 100s and dry conditions \nfrom Monday to Thursday. This will lead to widespread moderate and \nmajor HeatRisk. Heat products will likely be needed. The one \nuncertain factor is how much wildfire smoke will cool off the \ntemperatures. This will be highly dependent on where any new fire \nstarts occur and what the fire behavior will be which is outside our \narea of expertise. Conditions will be fairly dry in the afternoons \nwith fairly light winds Sunday and beyond. Will have to monitor for \nlightning mid to late next week as a trough offshore and monsoon \nhigh brings in PWATs around 120-170% of normal. Confidence in the \nexact trough positioning is low at this point but this pattern \nappears favorable for more lightning. /DB",
